PATCH 558: the deferred-object drain, the consumer patch 556 switched on.

`FUN_0000_c9af` is c33c's phase-6 handler and the CONSUMER of the deferred-object list that `c74d` and
`c715` append to.  Those two appenders were among board:0015's missing dispatch targets, so until patch
556 they resolved to nothing, the list was never populated, `bx >= count` held on the very first test
and c9af returned immediately.  With 556 landed the list fills, c9af walks it for the first time, and
its own base-losses become live -- AZER1 SEGVs one second into the mission:

    #0  FUN_0000_c9af (param_1=265604)  build/fist.c:34508
    #1  FUN_0000_c33c (si=0, di=27582)
    #2  FUN_0000_378e
    #3  FUN_0000_22dd
    #4  FUN_0000_459a

Bisected: with 556 removed AZER1 runs clean, with it in place it SEGVs, and 557 makes no difference.
This is the same shape as 553 -> 554: a correct patch makes dead code reachable and the code behind it
turns out never to have been reconstructed.  The fix is this function, not withholding 556.

The asm (0xc9af-0xca2e) is quoted in full at the site.  Four defects:

  (a) THE LIST IS IN STRSEG.  `c9b9: mov 0x70,%gs ; c9bd: mov %gs:0x366a(%bx),%si` -- the port had
      `*(int *)(DAT_2000_a6dc + 0x366a)`, i.e. the cursor added to a host pointer with no segment at
      all.  The port already performs this exact GS store correctly in c74d/c715 (556), 5fb0 (526) and
      a5dc, so the idiom was established.
  (b) BASE LOSS on the object.  `*(byte *)(iVar1 + 0x1b)` deref'd a DGROUP near offset as a host
      pointer; the asm is `test %al,0x1b(%si)`, DS-relative.
  (c) THE RECORD CURSOR.  `param_1` is ES:DI, and c33c dispatches its handlers with no arguments, so it
      was stack residue -- patch 554's class.  It is `g_fist_render_di`, advanced here by 0x32 exactly
      as `caab` and `ca2f` advance it (`add $0x2,%di` + 20 bytes of stores + `add $0x1c,%di`).
  (d) THE CARRY.  `ca10: clc` (drawn, c33c stops) and `ca2e: stc` (exhausted, c33c advances), both
      exits leaving SI=0xffff.  Ghidra dropped both.

Note the two stores at 0xc9f3/0xc9f5 write the SAME 32-bit value to [di+0xe] and [di+0x12]; that is
what the asm does (`66 ab` twice with no reload), not a transcription slip.

